[Event "Stafford Gambit"]
[Orientation "black"]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 {Petrov's Defence. The Stafford Gambit starts on move three, and only if White takes on e5. Every other third move is covered below with solid replies.}
3. Nxe5 {The only move that allows the gambit.}
(3. d4 {Petrov with 3.d4. Take the pawn back with ...Nxe4 and play for equality.} Nxe4 4. Nxe5 (4. Bd3 d5 5. Nxe5 (5. dxe5 Be7 6. O-O Nc5 7. Be2 O-O 8. Be3) 5... Nd7 6. Nxd7 Bxd7 7. O-O Be7 8. Re1) 4... d5 5. Nd2 (5. Bd3 Nd7 6. Nxd7 Bxd7 7. O-O Qf6 8. Bxe4) 5... Nd7 6. Qe2 Qe7 7. Nxe4 dxe4 8. Bf4)
(3. Nc3 {Four Knights. Develop with ...Nc6 and use the ...Nd4 idea against the Spanish.} Nc6 4. Bb5 (4. Bd3 Bc5 5. O-O (5. Nd5 d6 6. c3 Nxd5 7. exd5 Ne7 8. Bb1) 5... O-O 6. Nd5 d6 7. Nxf6+ Qxf6 8. c3) 4... Nd4 5. Nxd4 (5. Be2 d6 6. O-O c6 7. d3 Nxe2+ 8. Nxe2) 5... exd4 6. e5 dxc3 7. exf6 Qxf6 8. dxc3)
(3. Bc4 {Italian-style, aiming at f7. Take the pawn with ...Nxe4.} Nxe4 4. d3 (4. Nxe5 d5 5. Be2 (5. Bb5+ c6 6. Be2 Bd6 7. Nf3 O-O 8. O-O) 5... Bd6 6. Nf3 O-O 7. O-O Re8 8. d3) 4... Nc5 5. Nxe5 (5. Bg5 Be7 6. Bxe7 Qxe7 7. O-O d6 8. d4) 5... d5 6. Bb3 Bd6 7. O-O Bxe5 8. Re1)
(3. d3 {Quiet. Play ...Nc6 and ...d5 for a solid centre.} Nc6 4. g3 (4. c4 Bc5 5. Be2 (5. h3 a5 6. g3 d6 7. Bg2 h6 8. Be3) 5... d6 6. O-O Bg4 7. Kh1 a5 8. Nc3) 4... d5 5. exd5 (5. Qe2 Bc5 6. exd5 Nxd5 7. Bg2 O-O 8. O-O) 5... Nxd5 6. Bg2 Bd6 7. a3 a5 8. O-O)
3... Nc6 {Stafford Gambit. Give the e-pawn for fast development and a minefield of traps. Objectively dubious, practically very dangerous in blitz.}
4. Nxc6 {White accepts.}
(4. d4 Nxe5 5. dxe5 Nxe4 6. Qe2 Nc5 {White declines with 4.d4. Black regains the pawn and is solid but slightly worse.})
(4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. Qe2 Qe7 6. d3 Nf6 7. Nc3 d6 {White declines with 4.Nf3. A reversed French-style position that is roughly equal.})
(4. Nc3 Nxe5 5. d4 Ng6 6. e5 Ng8 7. Bc4 d5 8. Bxd5 {Four Knights style: this is the Halloween Gambit structure. Black is a piece up and must defend carefully.})
(4. Bc4 Nxe5 5. Bb3 d5 6. d4 Nc6 7. e5 Nd7 8. O-O {Take the knight and build the centre with ...d5.})
4... dxc6 {Always recapture with the d-pawn. It opens the d-file and the c8 bishop. 4...bxc6 gives no compensation.}
5. d3 {The most solid reply. White protects e4.}
(5. e5 Ne4 {Attack the centre.} 6. d4 (6. c3 Qh4 7. Qe2 (7. Qf3 Be6 8. d4 (8. d3 Nc5 9. d4 Nd7) 8... f5 9. Qf4 Qh5) 7... Nc5 8. d4 (8. g3 Qh6 9. Na3 Qg6) 8... Bg4 9. Qe3 Nd7) (6. d3 Bc5 7. dxe4 Bxf2+ 8. Ke2 Bg4+ {Trap. After 6.d3? Bc5! White loses the queen. 6.d4 and 6.c3 are better.}) 6... Be6 7. c3 (7. Be2 c5 8. d5 Bxd5 9. c4 Bc6) 7... f5 8. Bd3 h5 9. Qc2 Qe7)
(5. Nc3 Bc5 6. Bc4 (6. Be2 Qd4 7. O-O Nxe4 8. Nxe4 (8. Bf3 Nxc3 9. bxc3 Qf6 10. d4) 8... Qxe4 9. Bf3 Qh4 10. a4) (6. h3 Be6 7. d3 (7. Qf3 Qe7 8. d3 Bd4 9. Nd1) 7... Qe7 8. g3 O-O-O 9. Bg2) 6... Ng4 7. O-O Qh4 8. h3 Nxf2 {The engine prefers 8...Bxf2+, but this wins quickly if White errs.} 9. Qf3 Nxh3+ 10. Kh2 Nf2+ 11. Kg1 Qh1# {Trap: a long forced mate after White greedily castles.})
(5. Qe2 Bc5 6. h3 (6. c3 O-O 7. f3 (7. d4 Bb6 8. a4 a5 9. Bg5) 7... Re8 8. d4 Bd6 9. Qf2) 6... O-O 7. d3 Re8 8. g3 Nd5 9. Bg2)
(5. f3 Nh5 6. Qe2 (6. g3 f5 7. Qe2 (7. d4 f4 8. Rg1 c5 9. c3) 7... fxe4 8. Nc3 Be7 9. Nxe4) 6... Bc5 7. g3 (7. d3 Qh4+ 8. Kd1 g5 9. Bd2) 7... f5 8. c3 Qg5 9. e5)
5... Bc5 {Eyes f2 and prepares ...Ng4 and ...Qh4.}
6. Be2 {The safest move for White.}
(6. Bg5 Nxe4 7. Bxd8 Bxf2+ 8. Ke2 Bg4# {The famous queen trap. 6.Bg5?? loses at once.})
(6. Nc3 Ng4 {Threatens ...Nxf2 and ...Qh4. Black is doing very well here.})
(6. Be3 Bxe3 7. fxe3 Ng4 8. Qf3 Qh4+ {Black keeps the initiative.})
6... h5 {Prepares ...Ng4 and ...Qd6. The h-pawn becomes an attacking piece.}
7. O-O {Natural, but it walks into the fishing-pole trap.}
(7. Nc3 Ng4 8. Bxg4 hxg4 9. Qd2 Qh4 {White is somewhat better, but Black has play against the king.})
7... Ng4 8. h3 Qd6 {The key idea. Threat ...Qh2# or ...Nxf2.}
9. hxg4 (9. e5 Nxe5 {White's best, with a roughly equal game.}) 9... hxg4 {The h-file is open and mate cannot be stopped.}
